# Line endings are pinned because two things here compare bytes, not text.
#
# deps.lock stores a SHA-256 per vendored file, computed over the upstream
# bytes. A Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true rewrites those files on
# the way out, so scripts/update-deps.sh --check reports drift on files that
# never actually changed. CI runs on Linux and never sees it.
#
# scripts/update-deps.sh itself parses deps.lock with `read`, which leaves a
# stray CR on the blank line and aborts with "unrecognised key ''".

* text=auto eol=lf

# Vendored from psiberx/cp2077-cet-kit. Never rewrite these: the checksum in
# deps.lock is only meaningful against the bytes upstream published.
cet-kit/** -text

*.sh text eol=lf
deps.lock text eol=lf
